$O$ is the centre of a circle. Chord $CD$ meets $OP$ at $P$, with $OP\perp CD$. Prove that $\triangle OCP\equiv\triangle ODP$, and hence prove $CP=PD$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);\coordinate (C) at (-1.6,-1.2);\coordinate (D) at (1.6,-1.2);\coordinate (P) at (0,-1.2);
\draw (O) circle (2);\draw (C)--(D);\draw (O)--(P);\fill (O) circle (0.035);
\draw (0,-1.02)--(0.18,-1.02)--(0.18,-1.2);
\node[above] at (O) {$O$};\node[below left] at (C) {$C$};\node[below right] at (D) {$D$};\node[below] at (P) {$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Construct radii $OC$ and $OD$. In $\triangle OCP$ and $\triangle ODP$:
$\angle OPC=\angle OPD=90^{\circ}$ (given).
$OC=OD$ (radii of the same circle).
$OP$ is common.
Therefore $\triangle OCP\equiv\triangle ODP$ by RHS.
Hence $CP=PD$ because matching sides of congruent triangles are equal.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);\coordinate (C) at (-1.6,-1.2);\coordinate (D) at (1.6,-1.2);\coordinate (P) at (0,-1.2);
\draw (O) circle (2);\draw (C)--(D);\draw (O)--(P);\draw[dashed] (C)--(O)--(D);\fill (O) circle (0.035);
\draw (0,-1.02)--(0.18,-1.02)--(0.18,-1.2);
\draw (-0.86,-0.52)--(-0.74,-0.68);\draw (0.74,-0.68)--(0.86,-0.52);
\node[above] at (O) {$O$};\node[below left] at (C) {$C$};\node[below right] at (D) {$D$};\node[below] at (P) {$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
